---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Poddie Ops (POPS) — Max Vogt">
  <main id="top" class="page-container">
    <header class="section-pad fade-up">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-6">
        <label class="toggle text-base">
          <a class="text-[1.25rem] font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]" href="/">Max.</a>
          <input type="checkbox" aria-label="Toggle dark mode" data-theme-toggle />
          <span class="toggle-icons" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="toggle-icon icon-moon">◑</span>
          </span>
        </label>
        <nav class="flex items-center gap-8 text-base">
          <a class="nav-link transition-colors duration-200 hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]" href="/">Work</a>
          <a class="nav-link transition-colors duration-200 hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]" href="/about">About</a>
          <a
            class="nav-link transition-colors duration-200 hover:text-[var(--color-primary)]"
            href="https://www.instagram.com/maaxvogt/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            Fun
          </a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section-pad fade-up delay-1">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h1 class="text-[clamp(2.8rem,6vw,4.6rem)]">Poddie Ops (POPS) — A Full-Stack Operations Platform Built on the Edge</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-muted max-w-[52rem]">
          Poddie Ops is a custom, end-to-end operations management platform that runs an entire
          manufacturing business — from the moment a sales order lands to the day a modular office pod
          is produced, shipped, installed, and invoiced. It's built serverless-first on Cloudflare's edge
          network, integrates two external ERP/CRM systems (Katana MRP and Salesforce), and even
          gamifies the factory floor. One platform replaced a tangle of spreadsheets, manual hand-offs,
          and disconnected tools.
        </p>
        <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3 text-sm">
          <div>
            <p class="text-muted uppercase tracking-[0.2em]">Role</p>
            <p>Sole Architect & Developer</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-muted uppercase tracking-[0.2em]">Stack</p>
            <p>Cloudflare Workers, D1, Durable Objects, R2, React 18, TypeScript, TanStack Query, Vite, i18next</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-muted uppercase tracking-[0.2em]">Scope</p>
            <p>Backend, frontend, ERP integrations, infrastructure & ops</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad fade-up delay-2">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">The Problem</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted">
            Poddie manufactures modular office pods — configurable, physical products with dozens of
            options (exterior/interior colors, fire protection, sockets per country, furniture, monitors,
            and more). Running that business means orchestrating a long, branching chain of work: sales
            order → product configuration → serial-number assignment → multi-step production across two
            factory halls → logistics & route planning → on-site installation → invoicing & commission.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Each of those stages traditionally lived in its own silo — different spreadsheets, different
            people, no shared source of truth, and no real-time visibility. When something changed
            upstream (a delivery date, a configuration, a route), the information rarely reached the
            people downstream in time.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted">Poddie Ops was built to be that single source of truth.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">The Architecture</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted">
            Poddie Ops is split into two deployable sub-projects, both running on Cloudflare Workers:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-muted space-y-2">
            <li><span class="font-semibold">poddie-backend/</span> — a serverless API written in JavaScript on Cloudflare Workers, with 19 route handlers covering every business domain.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">poddie-frontend/</span> — a React 18 single-page app in strict-mode TypeScript, with 40+ routes across 16 feature areas.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Choosing the Cloudflare edge as the foundation was a deliberate bet: no servers to patch,
            global low-latency, and a tightly integrated data layer.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Compute</p>
          <p class="text-muted">Cloudflare Workers (nodejs_compat) — zero cold starts, deploys in seconds, scales to zero cost.</p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Database</p>
          <p class="text-muted">Cloudflare D1 (SQLite) — ~55 tables, 70+ versioned migrations, a real relational schema, not a key-value bolt-on.</p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Real-time</p>
          <p class="text-muted">A Durable Object, PoddieLive, runs a single WebSocket hub fanning out across 6 channels.</p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">File storage</p>
          <p class="text-muted">R2 buckets for product images, documents, and screenshots.</p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Auth</p>
          <p class="text-muted">Cloudflare Access (JWT) plus service tokens for enterprise SSO with an API-secret fallback for automation.</p>
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Scheduling</p>
          <p class="text-muted">Cron triggers handle ERP sync every 10 minutes and automated DB backups.</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            A detail I'm proud of: 70+ sequential SQL migrations. The schema wasn't designed once and
            frozen — it evolved alongside the business, every change captured as a reproducible, ordered
            migration applied directly to the production D1 database. That discipline is what kept a
            fast-moving project maintainable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">What It Actually Does</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted">
            Poddie Ops covers nine core business domains, each a fully-featured module:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-muted space-y-2">
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Orders & CRM</span> — customers, contacts, addresses, order creation, and two-way Salesforce opportunity sync.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Product Catalog</span> — products, configuration options, add-ons, per-country pricing, and production-step definitions.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Serial Numbers</span> — automatic, prefix-based serial generation and assignment with full audit logging.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Production</span> — units, multi-step workflows across two factory halls, team and employee assignment, machine benchmarking, and live progress logs.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Installation</span> — a stops-based routing system with calendars, teams, trucks, and signed delivery documents (CMR/LS).</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Invoices</span> — advance and final invoices, payment tracking, and sales commission.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Inventory</span> — stock management with reservation and sold-state tracking.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Unified Inbox</span> — tasks, forms, comments, and notifications in one place.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Dashboard</span> — KPIs and analytics across the whole pipeline.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-muted">
            The thread connecting all of these is real-time state. Through the PoddieLive Durable
            Object, a change made in production instantly reflects on the installation calendar, the
            dashboard, and any other connected screen — across channels for production, installation,
            orders, and per-user notifications.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">The Hard Parts (and How They Were Solved)</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">Bi-directional ERP integration with Katana MRP</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            This is the kind of integration that quietly breaks businesses. Poddie's manufacturing data
            lives in Katana MRP, but the team needed Poddie Ops to be the master and Katana the slave —
            a single, authoritative catalog.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            The solution: every catalog change in Poddie Ops (a new product, color, add-on value, or
            family) automatically triggers a background sync to Katana via Cloudflare's
            ctx.waitUntil(), plus a safety-net cron every 10 minutes. The sync maps Poddie's relational
            catalog into Katana's 15-group variant structure with a precise naming convention
            ("Product / model / version / color").
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted">Two engineering decisions stand out:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-muted space-y-2">
            <li>The sync only ever adds variants, never deletes them — protecting live sales orders from being orphaned by an upstream change.</li>
            <li>Graceful degradation: if the Katana API key is missing, the sync skips silently instead of throwing — the platform keeps working even when an external dependency is unavailable.</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-muted font-semibold mt-4">A schema that models a real, messy business</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            The 55-table D1 schema reflects genuine operational complexity — not a textbook example.
            Addresses are stored as immutable snapshots so historical orders stay accurate even when a
            customer moves. Serial numbers carry full provenance (manually changed by whom, when).
            Installation stops capture real-world details like elevator dimensions, building floor, and
            opening hours. This is domain modeling done by someone who understood the operation deeply.
          </p>

          <p class="text-muted font-semibold mt-4">Gamifying the factory floor</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            My favorite feature. To boost engagement on the production line, Poddie Ops runs a live
            Connect-4 game between production teams — every completed production step drops a token on a
            shared 6×7 board, with daily team pairings, cooldowns, and win detection. It's a small thing
            that turns routine work into a bit of friendly competition, powered by the same real-time
            WebSocket infrastructure as everything else.
          </p>

          <p class="text-muted font-semibold mt-4">A polished, human frontend</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            The React frontend isn't an afterthought. It's fully internationalized (German/English/Polish
            via i18next — important for a team spanning multiple countries), uses TanStack Query for
            robust server-state caching, and includes thoughtful touches like a personalized welcome
            screen with an online-colleague counter and a custom Vite plugin that auto-generates
            time-of-day celebration animations.
          </p>

          <p class="text-muted font-semibold mt-4">A standalone satellite service</p>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Beyond the core platform, I built poddie-reports — an independent, single-file Cloudflare
            Worker for employee bug/improvement/change reports. It shares the same D1 database, has a
            public submission form (with honeypot spam protection) and an admin dashboard, handles
            screenshot uploads to R2, and pixel-matches the main app's design tokens — all with no build
            step. It demonstrates that the platform is an extensible ecosystem, not a monolith.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">What This Project Demonstrates</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted">For anyone evaluating my work, Poddie Ops is evidence of:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-muted space-y-2">
            <li><span class="font-semibold">End-to-end ownership</span> — I architected, built, and operate the database, backend, frontend, integrations, and DevOps. There's no part of this stack I haven't touched.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Serverless / edge-native engineering</span> — real production use of Workers, D1, Durable Objects, R2, and Cloudflare Access, not a tutorial deployment.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Systems integration</span> — reliable, bi-directional sync with two third-party platforms (Katana MRP, Salesforce), with the failure modes thought through.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Real-time architecture</span> — a WebSocket hub built on Durable Objects that keeps an entire organization in sync.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Domain-driven design</span> — a 55-table schema and 70+ migrations that faithfully model a complex physical-manufacturing business.</li>
            <li><span class="font-semibold">Pragmatic product sense</span> — from immutable address snapshots to a Connect-4 game, decisions consistently balance correctness, resilience, and the human experience of the people who use the tool every day.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="case-row">
        <h2 class="case-title">Takeaway</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p class="text-muted font-semibold">
            Poddie Ops took a manufacturing operation that ran on spreadsheets and manual hand-offs and
            turned it into a single, real-time, integrated platform — built on modern serverless
            infrastructure, and running in production today.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-16 text-right">
        <a href="#top" class="nav-link text-base">Back to Top ↑</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h2>Let's get in touch</h2>
        <p class="text-muted">
          HMU for work, coffee, or if you just want to talk.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-6 text-sm">
          <a
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-transparent transition-colors duration-200 hover:border-[var(--color-primary)]"
            href="https://github.com/maaxvogt/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >
            GitHub
          </a>
          <a
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-transparent transition-colors duration-200 hover:border-[var(--color-primary)]"
            href="mailto:mavoxgt@gmail.com"
          >
            Mail
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">Designed and coded by Max Vogt</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

